 "response": "Kifux's current budget is **$948K**.\n\n**How I got there**\n\n1. **Valid sources.** Only Finance Office entries change the budget. Messages from marketing, vendors, legal, operations and other groups were ignored. Finance Office notes that are not budget changes were also ignored, such as audits, invoices and cost-center codes.\n\n2. **Withdrawals.** I resolved each withdrawal chain from the latest entry backwards. Each withdrawal that still stands voids the entry it targets. If that target is itself a withdrawal, the entry before it comes back into effect. Examples:\n   - #0003 (\u2212$461K) is withdrawn. The chain is #0837 \u2192 #0699 \u2192 #0422 \u2192 #0258 \u2192 #0006 \u2192 #0003. #0837 stands, so #0699 is void, #0422 stands, #0258 is void, and #0006 stands and withdraws #0003.\n   - #0005 (\u2212$434K) stands. The chain from #0887 back through #0528, #0375, #0226, #0213, #0146 and #0134 ends with #0075 being void.\n   - #0008 (\u2212$223K) stands. #0503 voids #0310, which restores #0178, which voids #0019.\n   - #0124 (\u2212$592K) stands. #1570 voids #0464, which restores #0354, which voids #0163.\n   - Other entries that end up withdrawn include #0011, #0004, #0094, #0565, #0228, #0759, #0139, #0448, #0313 and #0559.\n\n3. **Calculation.** I started from the $2,070K set in #0001. I then applied every remaining increase and decrease once, in record order.\n\nANSWER: $948K",
